{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1784646302,"nonce":"C8aIJw+CDEwOIeYcdHiDmnUbxUY=","message":"Root-cause analysis (no fix proposed).\n\nDiscovered revisions store the **first patch's subject** instead of the\n**cover-letter subject**, because cover-letter injection is skipped for them.\n\n`LoreMailbox.get_series()` injects the cover letter into `patches[0]`\n(`src/b4/__init__.py:387-404`), but it is only called for the primary/wanted\nrevision. `_record_discovered_revisions()` iterates *all* versions in the\nmailbox and derives each revision's stored subject by taking the first\nnon-None entry of the raw `LoreSeries.patches`\n(`src/b4/review/tracking.py:1150-1172`). For versions where `get_series()` was\nnever run, `patches[0]` (the cover) is not populated, so it falls through to\nthe first real patch's subject. That subject is written to the `revisions`\ntable and later shown in the list / used on upgrade (`update_series_revision`,\nsubject from `rev_info`).\n\nNet effect: series learned via revision discovery show the first patch's title\n-- most visible for Patchwork-tracked series, where discovery drives every\nversion.\n\nNote on \"recently\": the mechanism (skipping `get_series()` for discovered\nrevisions) is long-standing, so it is not obviously a fresh regression; a\nrecent change may simply have made it more visible. Worth a bisect against the\nexample series (msgid 87pl0hjg4g.wl-kuninori.morimoto.gx@renesas.com) to\nconfirm what changed the observed behaviour.","files":null}]}